@namespace SpaceSails.Client.Pages
@implements IHandleEvent
@*
    #251 · THE EMPTY-PLAN LINE AND THE PLAN-SHAPE WARNING, ON ITS OWN FILE.

    Moved out of NavHud.razor by PURE MOTION — the block between the two fences below is the block that
    was in the HUD, character for character. Every member it reads is a [Parameter] with the SAME NAME
    (fields keep their underscore, methods come across as delegates under their own names), which is what
    lets the markup move without a rewrite and lets MapMarkup splice it straight back where it came from.

    IHandleEvent repeats NavHud's own override, which repeats Map.Sim.cs's: the HUD's refresh belongs to
    OnTick's 200 ms throttle, and a child that did not repeat it would put an automatic render back on
    every event in here. The .razor.css beside this file carries no rules; it exists to give the component
    Map.razor.css's scope (pinned in SpaceSails.Client.csproj), so every rule that styled these elements
    goes on matching them.

    The `@{ }` around the fences is not decoration: the block came out of a Razor CODE block, and
    a code block is where its first statements are legal. It is outside the fences, so what the source
    guards read is still exactly the lines that were in the HUD.
*@
@{
@* ── MARKUP BEGINS · verbatim from NavHud.razor ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
                @* #989 · THE PLAN'S SHAPE, SAID AT THE DESK. Owner: "2 cast-off in sequence sounds kind of
                   silly — we should have some logic check." The check is Core's (CastOffRule.CheckShape),
                   and it is the SAME call that refuses a second pair at the button and that wakes the
                   captain once when a plan on the board goes bad. Here it is simply readable: a plan that
                   cannot be flown as written says so above the steps, in the rule's own words.

                   #997 · IT AND THE EMPTY LINE ARE BOTH HEAD, and that is the whole of the restructuring
                   this migration asked of the panel: what is above the plan does not scroll, the plan does.
                   The empty state is a head row for a reason a body element would have got wrong — a body
                   drawn on an empty plan would stand its own 6rem floor of blank scroller under a sentence
                   that says there is nothing to scroll. *@
                @if (PlanListIsEmpty)
                {
                    <div class="text-secondary">@(NavLockedByDock
                        ? "No steps yet. Press ⚓ + Cast off to start the plan at this berth, then add the burns and end it with an orbit or a dock."
                        : "No maneuver nodes. Scrub to a future time and add a burn — then end the plan with an orbit or a dock.")</div>
                }
                else if (PlanShapeWarningLine() is { } _shapeWarning)
                {
                    <div class="text-warning small mb-1">✗ @_shapeWarning</div>
                }
@* ── MARKUP ENDS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
}
